  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nContinued drier conditions will last through the remainder of the \nweekend and through most of next week in South Texas outside of \nisolated seabreeze showers and thunderstorms. Some residual energy \nfrom Hurricane Simon over in the Pacific will look to creep into the \nregion Monday with South Texas on the right flank of the area of low \npressure. Ahead of this onshore flow will return to the region \nallowing for deep moisture pooling beginning late this evening. The \npresence of these to factors will likely lead to a low chance for \nunsettled weather in the region Monday. Will continue to monitor \nthis over the next few model runs.\n\nHurricane Isaias made landfall a few hours ago, therefore the \nimpacts to South Texas will begin to wane. The threats will also \nbegin to wane with seas slowly returning to normalcy. Hazardous \nseas will last at least until midmorning today, though coastal \nflooding and rip currents will likely hold until Sunday morning. A \nCoastal Flood Advisory will remain in effect through then as well as \na high risk of rip currents. This is mainly due to higher swell \nperiods around 9-10 seconds which will gradually decrease through \nSaturday night.",
